WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our next guest is a talented
actor who you know

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.737
from his work as the
new Fresh Prince

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:07.774
on Peacock's most-streamed
original series, "Bel-Air."

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.876
All ten episodes are
streaming now on Peacock.

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:11.044
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:15.381
-There is a house
full of people in Bel-Air

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:16.883
that want you around, man.

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:21.621
All you gotta do
is get in your car and go back.

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.456
I promise, everything
will work itself out.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.291
-No.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:29.395
It's done.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.164
It's over.

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:34.633
You told me that this city
would try to make me forget

00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:37.604
who I am and where I come from.

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:44.177
And you were right.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:47.280
-Please welcome to the show,
Jabari Banks, everybody!

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.949
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:57.223 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.223 --> 00:01:04.497 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.465
Welcome to the show!
How are you?

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:07.600
-Oh, I'm great. Great.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.001
Excited to be here.
Thank you for having me.

00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:11.370
-I'm so happy to have you.
Congratulations on this show.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.139
For those who haven't seen it,
they might recognize

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.941
from the clip, this is more
a reimagining than a remake.

00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:19.078
This is a dramatic retelling
of the "Fresh Prince" story.

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:20.680
When you first heard
that they were doing that,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.914
what was your first reaction.

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:24.651
-I was like, "No. No. No way."

00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:26.252
And then I actually
saw the trailer

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.087
that Morgan Cooper
posted on YouTube,

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.923
and I watched it and I was like,
"Okay, I can get with this."

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.156
-Yeah.

00:01:31.156 --> 00:01:34.059
-And I couldn't imagine
that two years later,

00:01:34.059 --> 00:01:35.394
I'd be, you know, in this seat.

00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:38.765
-Now, I do have to point out
that you were not alive

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.000
when this show was on TV.
-No, no.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.637
-But what was your level of
familiarity with "Fresh Prince"?

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.573
-I am a "Fresh Prince
of Bel-Air" fanatic.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:48.975
My family had
the six-season box set.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.409
We have Will Smith
Christmas sweaters.

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:51.511
We love the show.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.279
-Okay, so you did know
the show, then.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.247
-Yes. Oh, for sure. For sure.

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:55.748
-And this is very interesting --

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.418
in real life, true story,
you have an Uncle Phil.

00:01:58.418 --> 00:02:00.119
-I do. His name is James Brogan.

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:03.456
-Okay, now, neither
of those names are Phil.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.458
-Right. No, no, no.
But he lives in Philly.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.327
I lived with him in Philly,
so we called him Uncle Phil.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.928
-Okay. Gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:11.964
-Are a lot of Uncle Phils
different people's names,

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:13.233
but they're from Philadelphia?

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.434
-Yeah, not really. Not really.
-Okay.

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.470
-He's the only one.
He's special to us.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.671
-I mean, it does
seem like those

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.574
are a lot of like sort of maybe,
you know,

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:24.076
harbingers that you would be
in a "Fresh Prince" one day,

00:02:24.076 --> 00:02:25.311
that you had an Uncle Phil

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:26.746
who wasn't
even a real Uncle Phil.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.213
-I know, right?
It's kind of strange, yeah.

00:02:28.213 --> 00:02:30.617
-And then it was your dad,
your dad was the one

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:33.186
who thought this
would be a good part for you.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:34.787
-Yes. He sent me an article.

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.255
He was like,
"They're casting for Bel-Air.

00:02:36.255 --> 00:02:37.991
You should do this.
Check it out.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:39.158
Call someone and figure it out."

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:41.127
And I was like,
"Okay, I'll try."

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.995
And then actually
a couple of months later,

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:45.030
my people actually
gave me the call.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.866
-'Cause your dad's
not in show business.

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.767
He just reads articles
and sends you ideas?

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:50.769
-Yeah, yeah, exactly.
Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:52.805
He's super supportive
and I love that.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.707
-I'm lucky enough to have
one of those as well.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.842
But now that
you've got the part,

00:02:56.842 --> 00:02:58.378
do you think he walks around
telling everybody,

00:02:58.378 --> 00:02:59.913
"You know this was my idea."

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:01.380
-Yeah.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.648
I'm sure he does.

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:03.816
He's actually in the audience
right now.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.517
-Oh, well, that's great!
Well, let me tell you.

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.520
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Shout out to my dad.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.921
Yeah.

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:11.057
-You go take all that credit.

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:12.725
-Exactly.
-You take all that credit.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:15.061
-Yeah.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.562
-That would be -- my dad
would take the credit,

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:17.663
if he pointed it out.

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:19.766
So you auditioned for it,

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:22.601
and you know, I think this was
one of those parts

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.003
where you cast a wide net.

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:26.305
You're trying to find, you know,
lightning in a bottle,

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:29.008
and tell us how you found out
you got the part.

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.877
-So Will actually called me
on FaceTime.

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:33.645
I actually got onto
the call thinking

00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:35.414
that it was an audition, right?

00:03:35.414 --> 00:03:36.748
So Morgan Cooper was like,

00:03:36.748 --> 00:03:38.784
"We want to do one more
creative session with you."

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:39.818
And I was like,
"What does that mean?"

00:03:39.818 --> 00:03:41.254
So I had all my sides lined up.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.189
I was like, "I'm gonna give
them everything that I have."

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:45.257
And I got on the call
and Will surprised me.

00:03:45.257 --> 00:03:48.027
And that was the moment
that changed my life.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:49.595
-Had you met him
through the process?

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:50.729
-No, no.

00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:52.165
-Oh, so the first time
you saw Will Smith

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.734
was him telling you that you
were going to be him in this.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.268
That's pretty cool.
-Yeah, it was very surreal.

00:03:56.268 --> 00:03:58.537
-That is a very cool thing.
-Yeah.

00:03:58.537 --> 00:04:01.440
My life literally got flipped,
turned upside down.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.542
-Yeah, it did get flipped,
turned upside down.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:05.077
And then how did you celebrate?

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:07.012
Is that news that you can
even celebrate or is it too big?

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:08.214
-Oh, no, I just went to sleep.
-You went to sleep?

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.548
-Yeah, I just went
straight to sleep.

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:11.884
Yeah. It was too much emotion
in my body. It was a lot.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.419
-I'm impressed that you
<i> could</i> go to sleep,

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.921
but that speaks well to your
ability to have an even keel.

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:16.990
-Oh, I can sleep anywhere.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.090
I could sleep right here
if I wanted to.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.226
-I'm like that, too.
Very good at that.

00:04:20.226 --> 00:04:22.862
Now you have -- you're
the youngest of five boys.

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.263
-Yes.

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:26.399
-How do they feel
about your success?

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.400
Are they as supportive as...
-Oh, they're so jealous.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.802
-Yeah? Good.
-No, I'm joking.

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.838
No, no, they're very supportive
and they love the show.

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:33.840
And we have a group chat
and they talk about it

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.041
all the time and they're like,
"No spoilers, no spoilers."

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:38.110
And I just love seeing
all the messages, yeah.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.913
-Did you guys, were you
as you grew up,

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:43.449
were you the kind of kids
who always got along,

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.884
or did you wail on each other?
-Oh, no.

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:47.253
Oh, a lot of fights during
the summer, but healthy fights.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:48.688
-And you're low man
on the totem pole,

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:50.856
so did you take it
pretty bad in back in the day?

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:52.358
-Yeah, I got it bad.
I got it real bad.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.726
-Alright, Good.
-Yeah, but that, you know,

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.260
that gave me the strength
to carry on, you know?

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.496
-Yeah. I'm glad.
I'm glad you rose above it.

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:59.732
You got nominated
for a BET Award for Best Actor.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:00.967
Congratulations.
That's a very cool thing.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:02.034
[ Applause ]
-Thanks.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.236
Thank you. Thank you.
Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:05.972
Super excited about that.
Yeah.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:09.875
-And you're gonna start shooting
a second season in September,

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:12.311
which is also great news.
-Yeah, Season 2 on the way.

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:13.746
[ Applause ]

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:15.014
-We were talking backstage,

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:16.682
'cause I would say, you know,
you got some time,

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:19.018
and you were telling me
you're going to work on music.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.319
So that's still
a passion for you?

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.255
-Yes, music was actually
my first passion.

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:23.656
And a lot of people ask me,
they're like,

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.491
"Do you love music more
or acting more?"

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.560
And I was like, it's like
choosing between your children.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.362
But yeah,
music was my first love,

00:05:30.362 --> 00:05:32.398
and I'm returning to it soon.

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:33.432
-So when you went to school,

00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:35.334
you went to, like,
an art school?

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.970
-An art school in Philadelphia.
University of the Arts.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.040
And I studied musical theater,
so music and acting

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.776
all at the same time,
yeah, and I loved it.

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:45.511
-When did you fall in love
with musical theater?

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.680
Was that something you found
in high school?

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:48.947
-Yeah, something I found
in high school.

00:05:48.947 --> 00:05:50.917
The first show
I did was "The Wiz,"

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.384
where I really
fell in love with it.

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:53.886
-Who were you in "The Wiz"?
-I was the lion.

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.455
-The lion?
-I wanted to be the scarecrow,

00:05:56.455 --> 00:05:58.590
but they didn't have
too many guys, you know,

00:05:58.590 --> 00:05:59.826
so they put me as a lion.

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:01.227
-And when you find out
you got the lion,

00:06:01.227 --> 00:06:04.396
did they have an actual lion
FaceTime you and tell you?

00:06:04.396 --> 00:06:05.998
-Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:08.234
It's dope, because my mom
actually made the costume.

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.268
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:06:09.268 --> 00:06:10.502
-My parents are very supportive.

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:12.671
-That's fantastic.
-She's in the audience too.

00:06:12.671 --> 00:06:14.373
[ Applause ]
-Here we go!

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:19.879
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:22.448
-I was a Wilbur in a production
of "Charlotte's Web."

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.485
-No way.
-This is a true story.

00:06:25.485 --> 00:06:27.286
You know, congratulations
on all your success

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:29.822
and further acting work.

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:31.390
But my mom said we read --
she goes,

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:34.260
"I found your old Wilbur
costume that we made."

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.661
And I was like, "Oh, I don't
really remember it."

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:37.830
And it was just a t-shirt,
and then there were, like,

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.566
iron-on letters
that spelled out "SOME PIG."

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:42.234
-Yeah, right, right.
-And I was like...

00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:43.802
I've got a feeling your mom
did a better job

00:06:43.802 --> 00:06:45.871
with your lion costume.
-Yeah. No, she did.

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:47.906
-And so is this something,
did your parents,

00:06:47.906 --> 00:06:48.874
did your older siblings,

00:06:48.874 --> 00:06:50.209
did they all know
from a young age

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:51.677
that this was the path
you were going to go on,

00:06:51.677 --> 00:06:53.212
that you were gonna follow this?

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:55.147
-Well, we all played sports
growing up,

00:06:55.147 --> 00:06:57.082
so we thought in some
form or fashion

00:06:57.082 --> 00:07:01.220
that we were going to be
in the league of...some league.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.789
No, they had no clue.
-Gotcha.

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:06.358
So you were good
at sports, too?

00:07:06.358 --> 00:07:08.427
'Cause a lot of
my coaches were like,

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:10.296
"You should do comedy."

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:11.797
-No, I was
pretty good at sports.

00:07:11.797 --> 00:07:13.399
-What was your sport?
What was your big one?

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:14.800
-Basketball and football.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.270
-Basketball. Loved basketball.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.004
I thought I was going
to go to the NBA.

00:07:19.004 --> 00:07:20.873
-But it does seem like

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:22.575
they're really supportive,
so when you tell everybody,

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:24.209
"Hey, I'm gonna go to this
performing arts college,"

00:07:24.209 --> 00:07:25.410
was everybody, like,
over the moon?

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:26.612
-Yeah, for sure. Yeah.

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:27.846
That was the only college
I got into.

00:07:27.846 --> 00:07:30.082
-Okay. [ Laughing ]

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:31.684
-But my parents, they made sure
I went to school.

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:32.951
-That's great.

00:07:32.951 --> 00:07:35.020
What were your other jobs,
before you get a job

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:36.989
as big as
being the Fresh Prince?

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:38.624
-I had a lot of different jobs,
actually.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.326
I worked at Six Flags
as Bugs Bunny.

00:07:40.326 --> 00:07:42.227
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:07:42.227 --> 00:07:44.096
-Hopefully, they made
the costume in that case.

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:46.164
-They did, yeah. For sure.

00:07:46.164 --> 00:07:47.533
I only worked there
for one day, though.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:48.667
-One day? What happened?

00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:50.035
-The eye holes were, like,
this big.

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:52.604
-Oh, you gave up the job.
-I gave up. Oh, yeah, for sure.

00:07:52.604 --> 00:07:54.373
It was in April. Yeah.

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:55.908
So it wasn't even
the summer yet.

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:57.242
-Yeah.
-It was going to get hotter.

00:07:57.242 --> 00:07:59.878
-It was a hot day at Six Flags?
-I was like, no way.

00:07:59.878 --> 00:08:01.780
But actually, before I got
the "Bel-Air" gig,

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:06.985
I was working
in a manufacturing factory

00:08:06.985 --> 00:08:08.387
in Temecula, California.

00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:10.890
-What were you doing
at the Temecula factory?

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.593
-I was wrapping gutters.
-Wrapping gutters?

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:15.461
So when you got,
when Will Smith called,

00:08:15.461 --> 00:08:16.962
was it
a situation where you said,

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:18.030
"Let me think about it?"

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.699
-Oh, no way.
No, no, no. Not at all.

00:08:20.699 --> 00:08:23.869
No, I had actually gotten fired
from that job.

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:25.804
I was working there for a month.

00:08:25.804 --> 00:08:28.940
I don't do well with,
you know...[ Laughing ]

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:30.943
And so this is a situation
where, do you think

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:33.379
the person who fired you
from the gutter wrapping job

00:08:33.379 --> 00:08:34.880
is aware
of what's happened since?

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.149
-Oh, I hope they are.
I hope they are.

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:40.386
I hope you're watching this.

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:44.656
[ Applause ]

00:08:44.656 --> 00:08:47.826
-The Bugs Bunny thing
is really interesting to me,

00:08:47.826 --> 00:08:50.395
because I didn't think the small
eye holes would be something --

00:08:50.395 --> 00:08:52.564
You feel like they would have
solved that problem.

00:08:52.564 --> 00:08:53.733
-It's awful. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:53.733 --> 00:08:55.334
-They have a small, little fan
in there,

00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:57.036
and they think that
that would suffice, but no.

00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:59.372
-Like a little electric fan?
-A little electric fan, yeah.

00:08:59.372 --> 00:09:00.973
-Okay, near your ear?
Is it like...?

00:09:00.973 --> 00:09:02.974
-It's like, somewhere up there.
I don't remember where.

00:09:02.974 --> 00:09:04.109
-And it doesn't
get the job done?

00:09:04.109 --> 00:09:05.377
-No, not at all.
-Yeah.

00:09:05.377 --> 00:09:07.280
-And I spent my whole paycheck
on the food there.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.448
-Okay.

00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:10.749
-Yeah, this is really not...

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:13.853
I feel bad that they're
our main sponsor tonight.

00:09:13.853 --> 00:09:15.187
[ Laughter ]

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:16.522
-Oh, God.

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:17.790
Shout out to Six Flags.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.292
-Sure, shout out to Six Flags.
I mean, obviously.

00:09:20.292 --> 00:09:22.761
I mean, I think a lot
of this was Jabari's fault.

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:24.063
-Yeah. Oh, for sure.

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:26.165
-Hey, man congratulations
so much on the show.

00:09:26.165 --> 00:09:27.667
It's really such a cool thing.

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:30.636
I think, like,
everyone, there was this,

00:09:30.636 --> 00:09:31.770
"What are they going
to do with it?"

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.172
And it's so cool
how they nailed the landing,

00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:34.739
and it's really amazing
that they found you.

00:09:34.739 --> 00:09:35.774
You're so great at it.

00:09:35.774 --> 00:09:36.808
Thanks for being here,
my friend.

00:09:36.808 --> 00:09:38.177
-Thank you, man.
-Really appreciate it.

00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:39.612
Jabari Banks, everybody!

00:09:39.612 --> 00:09:41.180
The entire first season
of "Bel-Air"

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:42.681
is streaming now on Peacock.

00:09:42.681 --> 00:09:44.550
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

